  "notes": "Umbundu, Guthrie R.11, Bantu language of the Ovimbundu of central-southern Angola. Five vowel qualities a/e/i/o/u each with a nasal counterpart (nasality on vowels is contextual, driven by adjacent nasal consonants, not written separately). Prenasalized stops (mb, nd, ndj, ng) are single phonemes. Two-register tone system (H/L): the standard orthography marks tone with an acute accent for high (á) on the first tone-bearing syllable of a word, and a grave accent for low (à); unmarked syllables continue the tone of the preceding syllable. Diacritics are not modelled as separate graphemes here; base-letter mappings are given.",
